  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Yet another very warm and humid day is expected \ntoday, followed by a gradual transition to cooler and drier \nconditions through the end of the work week. Much more comfortable \nweather is expected this weekend into early next week.\n\nOne more day of heat and humidity is on tap today, with 925mb \ntemperatures of +20C to +23C supporting highs well into the middle \nto upper 80s. Dew points in the lower to middle 60s will make it \nfeel warmer, with heat indices reaching the upper 80s to lower 90s. \nThis will result in Moderate HeatRisk, particularly along the I-95 \ncorridor, with those most sensitive to heat and those without \nadequate cooling or hydration most susceptible to impacts.\n\nThe pattern begins to change Wednesday as temperatures aloft \ngradually cool, with 925mb temperatures falling from the lower 20s \nCelsius today into the teens Celsius by Friday. Dew points will \nfollow suit, dropping from the 60s today into the 50s by late this \nweek. The result will be a noticeably cooler and more refreshing \nairmass by Friday. A quasi-stationary front will be the primary \ndriver of this transition. The front is expected to linger near \nsouthern New England late Wednesday into Thursday, keeping the \nregion in a more unsettled pattern. As the front finally pushes \noffshore Friday, as a cold front, drier air will filter into the \nregion behind it, aided by high pressure building southward from \ncentral Canada.\n\nThe weekend is shaping up to be the most comfortable stretch of \nweather in quite some time, with highs generally in the upper 70s \nacross interior southern New England and lower 80s along the coastal \nplain. Dew points in the 50s will make for much more comfortable \nconditions, while overnight lows fall into the 50s Saturday and \nSunday nights. These pleasant conditions may continue into early \nnext week, although the forecast becomes more unsettled by then.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Rain chances increase during the second half of the \nwork week as a frontal boundary settles across the region, followed \nby a return to dry weather for the weekend. Rain chances may \nincrease again early next week.\n\nDry weather and a good deal of sunshine are expected today, although \nskies may become somewhat filtered by elevated smoke from distant \nwildfires. Tonight, a weak system passing south of New England could \nproduce a shower or rumble of thunder across the extreme south coast \nand Islands, but probabilities remain low at less than 15 percent.\n\nWednesday remains a lower-confidence portion of the forecast. While \nmost guidance keeps a fast-moving low pressure system south of New \nEngland, there are some notable differences in the guidance. The 00z \nNAM develops a robust 995 mb surface low near the Benchmark \n(40N/70W), while the 3 km NAM also depicts a surface low in the \nvicinity, though considerably weaker. AI guidance likewise maintains \nthe surface wave south of New England. Given the overall consensus, \nthe current forecast favors a track south of the region, with little \nto no precipitation expected during the day Wednesday. The forecast \nbecomes more active Wednesday night into Thursday as a quasi-\nstationary front settles across the Northeast. Weak surface lows \nriding along the boundary will provide multiple opportunities for \nshowers, with precipitation chances increasing from late Wednesday \nnight through Thursday night. While periods of showers are likely, \nthe setup does not currently favor continuous rainfall across the \nregion.\n\nThe front should finally push offshore early Friday, allowing drier \nair to move into southern New England as high pressure builds \nsouthward from central Canada. This will usher in the dry and much \nmore comfortable conditions expected for the weekend. High pressure \nshould remain the dominant feature through the weekend, supporting \nsunny to mostly sunny skies, low humidity, and seasonable \ntemperatures. Beyond the weekend, the pattern becomes less settled, \nwith increasing rain chances possible early next week as the next \nsystem approaches.",
